  "solution": {
    "text": "&nbsp;&nbsp;Identify&nbsp;oxidation states of all elements in Redox Reaction<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mover><mtext>SO</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mover><mtext>Sn</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></mover><mo>+</mo><msub><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>O</mtext><mo>(l)</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mover><mtext>Sn</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></mover><mo>+</mo><mtext>H</mtext><mover><msub><msup><msub><mtext>SO</mtext><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo></msub><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mover><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mtext>H</mtext><mrow/><mo>+</mo></msubsup></math><br /><br />S is changing the oxidation state from&nbsp;(+4) to (+6). i.e H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub> is a Reducing agent because it undergoes oxidation."
